+---
+title: "Policy"
+description: "Policy implications of research on school attendance problems and emotionally based school avoidance."
+page-layout: full
+---
+
+::: page-header
+# Policy
+:::
+
+School attendance systems usually record how much school a child misses, but not consistently why. A policy response should make the reason for absence easier to record and use alongside attendance totals. That distinction can help schools identify distress-related absence earlier and match support to the problem being reported.
+
+## The research in brief
+
+::: paper-grid
+::: paper-card
+[Evidence]{.paper-label}
+
+### Intervention definitions
+
+The systematic review examines how intervention studies define attendance problems, identify targets, and measure success. Effects are easier to interpret when those three elements align.
+:::
+
+::: paper-card
+[Population]{.paper-label}
+
+### Reasons for absence
+
+The LSAC paper examines emotional symptom courses, school absence, and stated reasons across two Australian cohorts. The policy question is what is lost when systems record absence without its reason.
+:::
+
+::: paper-card
+[System]{.paper-label}
+
+### What existing data can see
+
+The third paper examines what school-held and child-level data can predict about attendance, and where those data remain unable to distinguish absence driven by distress.
+:::
+:::
+
+## Australian context
+
+This work sits alongside Australian attention to school refusal and attendance policy, including the [2023 Senate inquiry into school refusal](https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/Senate/Education_and_Employment/SchoolRefusal) and the Australian Education Research Organisation's work on [school attendance](https://www.edresearch.edu.au/research/projects/school-attendance). The [Victorian attendance policy](https://www2.education.vic.gov.au/pal/attendance/policy) illustrates how state policy defines attendance responsibilities and responses. State and territory policies differ, which makes clear operational definitions important for comparison and early support.
+
+## Work with me
+
+Departments, sector bodies, schools, and researchers working on attendance are welcome to get in touch about the evidence, data definitions, or policy implications of this work. [Contact me](/contact.html).
